From 89b5a8ede760fb1a112675d92193bb018a62cbb3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 13:00:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] read the alpha mask stored after a transparent bitmap

CorelDRAW stores a bitmap with transparency as its colour image followed
by an 8-bit alpha mask: an image record of its own with colour model 99,
the same width and height, and rows bottom-up like the colour data. It
starts a few bytes after the colour data (14 in every file seen).

readBmp stopped after the colour image, so the mask was never read: drop
shadows came out as solid black boxes, and cut-out overlays as opaque
sheets covering everything under them.

readBmp now looks for the mask header right after the colour data and
passes the mask to the new collectBmpAlpha. If the mask is not fully
opaque, CDRStylesCollector re-encodes the bitmap as RGBA PNG with zlib,
which is already a dependency, because consumers do not reliably read
BMP's fourth byte as alpha. Bitmaps with an opaque mask, and bitmaps
without a mask, stay BMP. The content collector now takes the MIME type
from the data instead of always writing image/bmp.
